        <h2 id="crimping">1. Precision Crimping: The Foundation of Pressure Retention</h2>
        <p>The crimping process is the primary mechanical operation that transforms a metal container into a pressurized delivery system. This stage involves the insertion of the valve into the can's 1-inch (25.4mm) opening, followed by the radial expansion of the valve cup. This expansion creates a permanent, airtight seal against the can's "curling" or rim. Because the internal pressure of an aerosol can often exceeds 5–8 bar at ambient temperature, the integrity of this seal is non-negotiable for shelf-life and consumer safety.</p>

        <div class="table-container">
            <table>
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th>Crimp Parameter</th>
                        <th>Technical Description</th>
                        <th>Impact of Failure</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Crimp Diameter</td>
                        <td>The final external measurement of the expanded valve cup within the can opening.</td>
                        <td>Insufficient diameter leads to "valve blow-out" under high temperatures.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Crimp Depth</td>
                        <td>The vertical distance from the top of the can rim to the engagement point of the crimp.</td>
                        <td>Incorrect depth causes improper actuator seating or failure to dispense.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Gasket Compression</td>
                        <td>The percentage of deformation (target 15-25%) applied to the valve seal.</td>
                        <td>Inadequate compression allows micro-leakage of propellant over time.</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <h2 id="gassing">2. Propellant Gassing: The Energetic Component</h2>
        <p>Once the container is sealed, the propellant — the "engine" of the aerosol — must be introduced. This is achieved through high-pressure injection systems that force the propellant into the can. In modern manufacturing, this is typically done using "Through-the-Valve" (TTV) technology, where the gassing head depresses the valve stem and injects the propellant at pressures higher than the can's internal resistance. For high-volume production, "Under-the-Cup" (UTC) gassing is used, where the propellant is injected just before the crimping head seals the valve.</p>

        <img src="https://assets.zyrosite.com/A3QlOV94WLSaL6xE/ttc-and-utc-filling-pOgVMlUsFotcs3Kh.png"
             alt="Technical diagram of TTV vs UTC gassing methods"
             loading="lazy" width="1280" height="720">
        <p class="img-caption">Technical diagram showing the difference between TTV (Through-the-Valve) and UTC (Under-the-Cup) gassing methods</p>

        <h2 id="weight-check">3. Automatic Weight Checking: The Quality Auditor</h2>
        <p>In an automated aerosol line, the weight check station serves as the final arbiter of success. Because the propellant is injected at high speeds, even a momentary drop in line pressure can result in an under-filled can. Conversely, an over-filled can is a safety hazard, as it may lack the "headspace" required for the propellant to expand if the can is exposed to heat. Modern check-weighers use EMFR technology to monitor every unit without stopping the production line.</p>

        <h2 id="leak-detection">4. Leak Detection: The Safety Firewall</h2>
        <p>Aerosol cans are essentially pressure vessels. International transport regulations (such as ADR or DOT) and safety standards (like PESO in India) require that every single can produced be tested for leaks. This "safety firewall" prevents defective units from entering the supply chain, protecting both the consumer and the brand's reputation.</p>

